 "tempo": 1.06,
 "id": "Pencil",
 "slug": "pencil",
 "topic": "How the pencil was invented (story Reel drawn in code as pencil sketches on notebook paper)",
 "title": [
  "WHO INVENTED",
  "THE PENCIL"
 ],
 "accent": "PENCIL",
 "script": [
  {
   "beat": "s",
   "text": "The pencil was invented by a storm. And a flock of sheep."
  },
  {
   "beat": "s",
   "text": "This ain't some bedtime story, buddy. Let me sketch it out for ya."
  },
  {
   "beat": "s",
   "text": "England. Fifteen sixty-four, or thereabouts. A storm rips a big old tree clean out of the ground."
  },
  {
   "beat": "s",
   "text": "And under the roots, shepherds find a strange black rock. Soft. Shiny. And it writes."
  },
  {
   "beat": "s",
   "text": "So they use it to mark their sheep."
  },
  {
   "beat": "s",
   "text": "Folks figure it's some kind of lead. It ain't. Never was. There has never been lead in a pencil."
  },
  {
   "beat": "s",
   "text": "That black rock got so valuable, they guarded the mine with guns. And smugglers snuck it out in their pockets."
  },
  {
   "beat": "s",
   "text": "But it was messy. So they wrapped it in string. Then somebody in Italy slid it inside a stick of wood."
  },
  {
   "beat": "s",
   "text": "Then, seventeen ninety-five. France is at war, and can't get the English rock."
  },
  {
   "beat": "s",
   "text": "So a fella named Cont\u00e9 grinds up the scraps, mixes them with clay, and bakes them like biscuits. That's the pencil you got today."
  },
  {
   "beat": "s",
   "text": "You get it, buddy? I said, you get it?"
  },
  {
   "beat": "s",
   "text": "And in eighteen fifty-eight, a man named Lipman sticks an eraser on the end. Because everybody makes mistakes."
  },
  {
   "beat": "s",
   "text": "They say one pencil can draw a line thirty-five miles long. That's real."
  },
  {
   "beat": "s",
   "text": "So next time you chew on one, remember. You're chewing on a storm."
  },
  {
   "beat": "s",
   "text": "That's about it, folks. Follow for more strange history. See ya!"
  }
 ],
 "science": [
  {
   "claim": "Graphite found at Borrowdale, Cumbria, around 1564 after a storm felled trees; used to mark sheep",
   "note": "traditional account; date hedged"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Pencils never contained lead (graphite was mistaken for a lead ore, \"plumbago\")",
   "note": ""
  },
  {
   "claim": "The mine was guarded and graphite smuggled; theft made a felony in 1752",
   "note": ""
  },
  {
   "claim": "Conte, 1795: powdered graphite mixed with clay and kiln-fired",
   "note": "patent 1795"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Hymen Lipman patented the pencil with an attached eraser in 1858",
   "note": "patent later invalidated"
  },
  {
   "claim": "One pencil can draw a line about 35 miles long",
   "note": "commonly cited figure; hedged with \"they say\""
  }
 ],
